2. GADS (https://github.com/shamanec/GADS), read at tag v6.0.0
--------------------------------------------------------------

GADS has two licences. Its LICENSE-OVERVIEW.txt says:

    "Except as noted below, all files in this repository are licensed under
     the GNU Affero General Public License v3.0."
    "The following components are proprietary and licensed under the GADS
     Proprietary License: - hub-ui"

* LICENSE in the GADS repository is the AGPL-3.0 text.
* PROPRIETARY-LICENSE.txt ("GADS PROPRIETARY LICENSE", last updated
  23/MAY/2025) covers hub-ui, the hub's web interface. It grants "a
  non-exclusive, non-transferable, limited license to use the proprietary
  components of GADS ... solely for your internal business purposes" and
  forbids, among other things, to "copy, modify, ... decompile, disassemble,
  or reverse engineer" it and to "distribute, sublicense, lease, rent, loan,
  or otherwise transfer the Proprietary Software to any third party".
* hub-ui is a git submodule that points at a private repository; its source
  is not public. Upstream's release binaries embed a built, obfuscated copy.

What this kit does with each part:

a) The hub. The installer downloads the publisher's own release file
   (of v6.0.0: gads-win.zip with install.ps1; gads-mac-arm64.zip or
   gads-mac-amd64.zip with install.sh) directly from the publisher's GitHub
   release page to the farm computer, and verifies its SHA-256. This repository and its
   branches do not contain, mirror or modify that file. The person running a
   farm uses it on their own computer for their own business; whether that
   use fits the proprietary licence of the embedded hub-ui is for that person
   to judge from the licence text quoted above.

b) The provider. The branch "dist" of this repository holds the patched
   provider as three programs built from the same source: GADS-drag.exe
   (Windows x64), GADS-drag-darwin-arm64 and GADS-drag-darwin-amd64 (macOS),
   in one folder per provider version. They are built on a maintainer's
   computer with tools/build-provider.sh, from the AGPL-3.0 source of GADS at
   the pinned commit plus patch/provider-drag.patch; the build is
   reproducible, and the folder carries the checksums and the build
   instructions, so that anybody can build the programs again and compare.
   What follows is true of each of them.
   - It is built WITHOUT the "ui" build tag, so it contains no hub-ui code.
     (In GADS, embed_ui.go embeds hub-ui/build only under that tag;
     no_embed_ui.go is used otherwise.)
   - It is distributed under AGPL-3.0. The same folder of the branch
     carries, next to the binary:
       LICENSE-AGPL-3.0.txt      the licence;
       GADS-drag-<version>-source.tar.gz
                                 the patched GADS tree, the generated docs
                                 package, the sources of every Go module the
                                 program is built from (vendor/), the patch,
                                 BUILD.md (Go version, tools and the exact
                                 build command) and MODIFICATIONS.md (what
                                 was changed, by whom, and the date);
       BUILD.md, MODIFICATIONS.md
                                 the same two files, for reading without
                                 unpacking the archive;
       THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt   the licence, notice and copyright files of
                                 the Go modules that are compiled into the
                                 three programs (MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0 and
                                 others; a few modules are compiled into
                                 the program for one system only),
                                 which those licences require to accompany a
                                 binary. The Go standard library and runtime
                                 are BSD 3-Clause (https://go.dev/LICENSE).
     One thing needed to build is NOT in the source archive: the folder
     resources/ios-ddi (next point). The archive names its three files with
     their SHA-256 and the upstream address to fetch them from (in
     IOS-DDI-NOT-INCLUDED.txt, at the top of the tree). The archive
     is therefore the complete source except for those three files.
   - Like every GADS binary, it embeds the "resources" directory of the GADS
     repository: gads-broadcast.ipa and gads-settings.apk (GADS's own helper
     apps), three zsign executables (https://github.com/zhlynn/zsign), and an
     iOS developer disk image with its manifest and trust cache
     (resources/ios-ddi). The provider needs the disk image at run time for
     iPhones on iOS 17 and later, so it cannot be left out of the binary.
     GADS's LICENSE-OVERVIEW.txt places all files other than hub-ui under
     AGPL-3.0; this kit relies on that statement and has not examined the
     origin of those files further. In particular, the developer disk image
     is Apple software as far as we can tell, and the .ipa, .apk and zsign
     files are pre-built: the source archive carries them as upstream ships
     them, without their source.
   - Publishing these programs therefore makes this repository a distributor
     of those embedded files, as upstream's own releases are. That is a
     decision for the people who publish, and the tools do not take it for
     them: tools/publish-dist.sh publishes nothing until versions.json says
     "redistributeEmbeddedResources": true (docs/RELEASING.md). If the file
     is ever withdrawn, a farm that has it keeps working, and the install and
     update scripts accept a local copy (-ProviderExe on Windows,
     --provider-binary on macOS; checked against the pinned SHA-256). An alternative that redistributes nothing is to build
     the provider on each farm computer from upstream source and the patch
     (patch/README.md).

c) AGPL-3.0, section 13 (remote network interaction). Section 13 asks that
   a modified version which people use over a network must itself offer
   those people its source. The patch does that in the program: every answer
   of the modified provider carries the HTTP header
       X-Source-Code: https://github.com/fyhcrew-art/blossom-farm-kit/tree/dist
   (provider/router/handler.go in the patch), and each folder of that branch
   carries the source archive described under b). The provider gate passes the header
   on unchanged. A farm operator who runs the provider as this kit installs
   it has nothing more to do. An operator who changes the provider further
   and lets others use it over a network must publish that source and point
   the header (the constant sourceOffer) at it.

3. Programs the installer fetches (not part of this repository)
---------------------------------------------------------------

On Windows: installed with winget from the publishers' own download
locations, at the versions pinned in versions.json. Licences as stated by the
projects:

* MongoDB Community Server (MongoDB.Server) - Server Side Public License v1
* MongoDB Database Tools (MongoDB.DatabaseTools) - Apache License 2.0
* Node.js (OpenJS.NodeJS.LTS) - MIT and bundled third-party licences
* FFmpeg, "full" build by Gyan (Gyan.FFmpeg) - GPL v3 for that build
* cloudflared (Cloudflare.cloudflared) - Apache License 2.0; use of the
  Cloudflare service is under Cloudflare's terms
* NSSM (NSSM.NSSM) - public domain; the installer keeps a private copy of
  nssm.exe in C:\farm\bin on the farm computer

On macOS: downloaded by install.sh from the publishers' own download
locations into /opt/farm/bin, at the versions and with the SHA-256 values
pinned in versions.json:

* Node.js (nodejs.org) - MIT and bundled third-party licences
* MongoDB Community Server (fastdl.mongodb.org) - Server Side Public
  License v1
* MongoDB Database Tools (fastdl.mongodb.org; only when a farm is exported
  or imported) - Apache License 2.0
* cloudflared (github.com/cloudflare/cloudflared) - Apache License 2.0; use
  of the Cloudflare service is under Cloudflare's terms

and installed with Homebrew, which the person running the farm installs
themselves (https://brew.sh, BSD 2-Clause), at the version Homebrew offers
on that day:

* FFmpeg (formula "ffmpeg") - GPL v3 or later as Homebrew builds it
* OpenSSL 3 (formula "openssl@3"; on Apple Silicon, used by the signing
  helper inside the GADS hub) - Apache License 2.0

Only with Appium ("install.ps1 -WithAppium", "install.sh --with-appium"),
installed with npm from the npm registry:

* Appium (appium) and its iOS driver (appium-xcuitest-driver) - Apache
  License 2.0 - and the GADS Appium plugin (appium-gads, part of GADS). The
  versions of these three packages are pinned in versions.json. The packages
  they depend on (several hundred, under their own licences) are resolved by
  npm at installation time and are neither pinned nor listed here.

Not installed by the kit, but required for iPhones (docs/phones.md):

* On Windows: iTunes for Windows (Apple) - Apple's licence terms. On macOS
  nothing: Apple's USB support for iPhones is part of the system
* WebDriverAgent, fork by the GADS author
  (https://github.com/shamanec/WebDriverAgent) - BSD licence of
  WebDriverAgent. The farm operator downloads the pre-built file from that
  repository's own release page and signs it with their own Apple Developer
